  "content": {
    "id": "p37-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "A fair coin is tossed five times. Find the probability that:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p37-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "exactly two heads are obtained, given that the first toss is heads.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac14$",
          "worked": "Given that the first toss is heads, exactly one of the remaining four independent tosses must be heads. Let $Y$ count heads in those four tosses.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(Y=1)&=\\binom41\\left(\\frac12\\right)^1\\left(\\frac12\\right)^3\\\\\\Pr(Y=1)&=\\frac4{16}\\\\\\Pr(Y=1)&=\\frac14.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "the first toss is heads, given that exactly two heads are obtained.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac25$",
          "worked": "Given exactly two heads, their positions form $\\binom52$ equally likely pairs. If the first toss is heads, the other head can occupy any one of the remaining four positions. Let $H_1$ mean that the first toss is heads and $X$ count the total heads.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(H_1\\mid X=2)&=\\frac{\\binom41}{\\binom52}\\\\\\Pr(H_1\\mid X=2)&=\\frac4{10}\\\\\\Pr(H_1\\mid X=2)&=\\frac25.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      }
    ]
  },
